<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Ikhshidid emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">935 CE – 969 CE · capital Fustat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An autonomous dynasty that ruled Egypt, Syria and the holy cities under Abbasid sovereignty, ending with the regent Kafur.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-tughj-al-ikhshid">Muhammad ibn Tughj</a>, a commander of Central Asian descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Farghana</a>, became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 935 and restored order after years of turmoil. In 939 the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> granted him the old princely title al-Ikhshid, which gave the dynasty its name, while he ruled as an autonomous emir of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Ikhshidids governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> and held southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hejaz">Hejaz</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, contesting northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-tughj-al-ikhshid">al-Ikhshid</a> died in 946, real power passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-misk-kafur">Abu al-Misk Kafur</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> of African origin, who ran the state as regent for two sons and ruled in his own name from 966. The poet al-Mutanabbi spent time at his court, first praising and then lampooning him.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-misk-kafur">Kafur</a>'s death in 968, famine and factional strife left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> open to attack. In 969 the Fatimid general <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jawhar-state">Jawhar</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> almost unopposed and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> nearby, bringing Egypt into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ikhshidid-emirate.jpg" alt="Ikhshidid emirate" width="320" height="153"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ikhshidids._Muhammad_al-Ikhshid.Filastin_(al-Ramla)_mint._Dated_AH_332_(943-4_CE).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>935 CE – 969 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fustat</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad ibn Tughj al-Ikhshid, Abu al-Misk Kafur</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>, <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ikhshidids, Ikhshidid dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=952&amp;focus=ikhshidid-emirate" aria-label="Ikhshidid emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5492 1082 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ikhshidid emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5812 1338L5811 1337M5812 1338L6134 1224"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5811 1337h0M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5812 1338h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5812 1338h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=952&amp;focus=ikhshidid-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
